of lab  confirmed suspected and possible  cases of coven 19 and building on a lot  of  initiatives so i stole this slide off  their website  that shows um things you see odyssey  there  i2b2 p cornet and then you see the  hl7 fire truck going down the middle  because the data is all standards-based  so um basically those who participate  in n3c enter into a data use agreement  which has to go through the  institutional irb  they can then load data into the data  enclave  so the data is a centralized resource  it's  protected and  in order to use it you have to fill out  a data use request you have to have  various research trainings  and your projects get approved um  interestingly enough there's three  levels of data  there's the hipaa safe harbor which is  the most  um it doesn't really it has  some identifiers that that are part of  phi  protected health information um so you  you have to  get the most um or i'm sorry that's the  i'm sorry i got that wrong  that's the limited data set the one at  the bottom the hipaa safe harbor  is fully de-identified the limited data  set is mostly de-identified but has  potential for re-identification  um and then they're also creating a  synthetic data set  that that won't be real patient data but  it'll have the characteristics of the  real patient data  obviously it will be there'll be more  hoops to jump through to get  to the limited data set than the say the  synthetic data set and all the data will  need to be used in the  enclave there's a phenotyping  that has been developed so the data gets  standardized as it comes in from each  institution  and um the link to that is here  um so again i hope um the n3c folks will  come back and tell you more i have to  say i i've actually one of uh  our master's students has an interest in  working with um  atypical mycobacterium infections in  covet 19. so i'm gonna  i'll be his mentor and get to uh play  around in the  data enclave a little bit myself um i  mentioned open safely  again the reason why i like i i find  this project  interesting is they they were really  able to show all the risk factors that  we  now know all too well about um  complications and mortality from covet  19  higher age groups um co-morbid  conditions  certain ethnicities and um  you know fortunately this demonstrates  the value of having large  standardized data sets and um there will  probably be a lot to learn especially as  we start  uh carrying out uh effective treatments  immunizing the population and so forth  so i i will follow those studies  another area of interest to those of us  in informatics is open science  so making scientific information as  accessible as possible  um covet 19 has actually tested  uh that approach i think we're all still  firm advocates of open science but we've  seen some of the limitations  and part of it has to do with the  politics and the 24-hour news cycle  so as a result um things  make it into the news cycle they may be  poor quality research  there may need to be media there may be  that may lead to media hype  all the partisanship we have political  partisanship  and and that has led to proliferation  of outright pseudoscience conspiracy  theories  about the virus about certain types of  treatments and so forth  part part of the problem has been  exacerbated by something that we think  is a great thing in open science which  is pre-prints so  we no longer have to wait until an  article is  accepted to share it with our colleagues  we can put it on pre-print servers it  has a url so people can get to it  but the pre-print servers have been  inundated with  mixed quality research some good some  really bad  quality research um and in fact not only  that  um journals have been quick to accept  papers to get the word out  um and then now unfortunately though  there's a growing list of retracted  papers where  either the researchers themselves or  others may find real  challenges in in the research and some  of you may enjoy the  retraction watch blog that  deals with retracted papers we know that  information out there i mean we've known  this problem about the world wide web  almost since the web started the  variable information quality  one analysis looking at covid19  information found that in general it was  better on  oregon.edu sites than com but there's  plenty of variation  um the also um another challenge of  science with covid19  is that the information um uh  there's just this quick reaction we got  to do a clinical trial for this do do it  for that without a lot of coordination  um leading to waste and duplication as  noted by some writers  but still it's important to preserve the  integrity of clinical trials i think we  see that playing out with  vaccines and there is a role for  observational studies so  non-experimental studies but  those must also be  paired with or followed up by or based  on randomized control trials  we all know that covid19 has exposed  many fault lines  in our society issues around um  systemic racism income equality even  climate change  and um how has it done this um well  some not all these actually have to do  with coven 19 proper but it's exposed  these sorts of things  so for example as we um use more and  more  health care data to allocate resources  if we have health care disparities that  are baked  into data like shown in the study by  obermeyer in science  last year where african american  patients received  less care  and so that got baked into the data and  baked into the  machine learning algorithms that were  used to recommend allocations for care  we need to protect data there's lots of  people that want to monetize our data  maybe there's a role for that  but really the person the patient  really is the one who should have the  say over what is done with their data  we um many clinical algorithms have  historically used  race and sometimes that's appropriate  but  other times not there's a nice paper in  the new england journal of medicine  about that earlier this year  our old friend peter embi alumnus of the  program now  director of the regan street institute  um  wonders whether we need algorithmo  vigilance  not not only as algorithms are developed  but how they may  change when um there's they start to be  used which will necessarily change the  data  um and then a a covet 19 specific  this was a study looking at how relief  funding was allocated to hospitals  from the federal legislation  and it noted that  money was allocated based on historical  revenues now  we know that um the hospital's revenues  are not  necessarily indicative of of what they  do they may have higher revenues because  they have a different  payer mix and things like that so all of  these things i i mentioned  um just show that we really need to be  thoughtful about how not only how we use  data  but how we collect it what we choose to  measure what we ignore  and and i think the connection to cobit  19 is that  is that coven 19 has just exposed a lot  of these problems  so i am going to stop at this point  i um met my intended target for time so  i'm going to stop  my screen sharing so i can see the rest  of my  webex window and so i am  um happy to answer any questions  um looking at the um  question box uh i see a few announcement  type questions there  um and yes ben orwell did give a talk on  information blocking that i hope he'll  he'll give to us it's a complicated  topic  um i'll be happy to um  either people can type into the chat box  or  feel free to unmute yourself if you  have any comments or questions about  what i've talked about  this is always one of the challenges  with webex is that  i can't see any you other than than your  [Music]  names  well if there are no questions um we can  uh wrap up the session and um  i just want to make a reminder to those  attending the open house that will  resume um that session  at 12 30 and i'll be back to kick that  off and then we  we have a whole variety of speakers  including a panel of students from the  program to talk to you all  so um i want to thank you all um i want  to encourage other people to  um give your talks at this conference  so we can maybe go into some of these um  areas in more detail and um i will see  some of you  in the session uh in the open house  session that starts in about  um uh 12 minutes thank you everyone
 

